From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Apex <apex@localhost>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:00:00 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] elan: Never fail a 04f3:0c6e capture on its pre-scan answer

On an installed system with 0001 and 0002, enrollment on the 0x0c6e
still ends at once with FP_DEVICE_ERROR_PROTO, during the identify that
fprintd runs before an enrollment:

  Device reported an error during identify for enroll: The driver
  encountered a protocol error with the device.

With 0001 and 0002 the only source of that error in a capture is the
last branch of CAPTURE_READ_DATA: a pre-scan answer other than 0x55,
0x00 and 0xaf, or a read that returned no byte. Debug logs from other
0x0c6e units (libfprint#808, #916) show when it happens: a capture
that starts right after the previous one, with the finger still on the
power key while calibration takes the background. Calibration then
accepts a background with a delta of 170 to 225 instead of about 30,
and the next pre-scan read blocks for most of a second before it
answers something other than 0x55. Those logs come from builds without
0001, so they do not show the byte.

For ELAN_RETRY_UNEXPECTED_STATUS devices (0x0c6e), handle any other
answer, and an empty read, the way 0002 handles 0x00 and 0xaf: after a
frame it means the finger was lifted; before one it is retried with the
same back-off, and a run of such answers ends after ELAN_CMD_TIMEOUT
as a read timeout would. The session therefore never ends with a
protocol error on this device, and fprintd retries the scan instead of
reporting enroll-disconnected.

The first unexpected answer of a run is logged as a warning, which
fprintd's journal keeps without G_MESSAGES_DEBUG, so the byte this
sensor sends shows up in the next report. A read without a byte is
logged as 0x100.

No other device changes behaviour.

Refs: libfprint#808 #916 #879
---
 libfprint/drivers/elan.c | 19 +++++++++++++++++--
 libfprint/drivers/elan.h |  7 +++++++
 2 files changed, 24 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libfprint/drivers/elan.c b/libfprint/drivers/elan.c
index 03576d5..cd301c0 100644
--- a/libfprint/drivers/elan.c
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/elan.c
@@ -557,6 +557,11 @@ enum capture_states {
  * path. Nothing may complete the SSM while the delay is pending:
  * fpi_ssm_mark_completed () does not refuse, it logs a BUG_ON critical,
  * drops the pending jump and completes the SSM. */
+/* The pre-scan answer for log lines: the byte, or 0x100 for a read that
+ * returned no byte at all. */
+#define ELAN_PRE_SCAN_ANSWER(self) \
+  ((self)->last_read ? (unsigned int) (self)->last_read[0] : 0x100u)
+
 static void
 elan_transient_status (FpiSsm *ssm, FpiDeviceElan *self)
 {
@@ -570,7 +575,7 @@ elan_transient_status (FpiSsm *ssm, FpiDeviceElan *self)
   if ((self->dev_type & ELAN_TRANSIENT_STATUS_LIFT) && self->num_frames > 0)
     {
       fp_dbg ("pre-scan status 0x%02x after %d frames: finger lifted",
-              self->last_read[0], self->num_frames);
+              ELAN_PRE_SCAN_ANSWER (self), self->num_frames);
       fpi_ssm_mark_completed (ssm);
       return;
     }
@@ -598,7 +603,7 @@ elan_transient_status (FpiSsm *ssm, FpiDeviceElan *self)
 
   self->transient_retries++;
   fp_dbg ("transient pre-scan status 0x%02x: retry %u in %d ms",
-          self->last_read[0], self->transient_retries, delay_ms);
+          ELAN_PRE_SCAN_ANSWER (self), self->transient_retries, delay_ms);
   fpi_ssm_jump_to_state_delayed (ssm, CAPTURE_WAIT_FINGER, delay_ms);
 }
 
@@ -639,6 +644,16 @@ capture_run_state (FpiSsm *ssm, FpDevice *dev)
            * 0xaf - finger removed or sensor busy (seen on 0x0c58 and 0x0c6e) */
           elan_transient_status (ssm, self);
         }
+      else if (self->dev_type & ELAN_RETRY_UNEXPECTED_STATUS)
+        {
+          /* any other answer, or a read that returned no byte: handled as a
+           * transient answer, so it is bounded and never a protocol error */
+          if (self->transient_retries == 0)
+            fp_warn ("unexpected pre-scan status 0x%02x after %d frames: "
+                     "handling it as transient",
+                     ELAN_PRE_SCAN_ANSWER (self), self->num_frames);
+          elan_transient_status (ssm, self);
+        }
       else
         {
           /* XXX: The timeout is emulated incorrectly, resulting in a zero byte read. */
diff --git a/libfprint/drivers/elan.h b/libfprint/drivers/elan.h
index 1e866f6..3d79121 100644
--- a/libfprint/drivers/elan.h
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/elan.h
@@ -61,6 +61,13 @@
  * so an idle sensor that keeps answering cannot poll at 100 Hz. */
 #define ELAN_TRANSIENT_STATUS_BACKOFF ELAN_0C6E
 
+/* devices on which any other pre-scan answer, or a read that returned no
+ * byte, is handled like 0x00 and 0xaf instead of failing the session with
+ * FP_DEVICE_ERROR_PROTO. The 0x0c6e answers something else when the capture
+ * starts soon after the previous one, with the finger still on the sensor
+ * while calibration takes the background (libfprint#808, #916). */
+#define ELAN_RETRY_UNEXPECTED_STATUS ELAN_0C6E
+
 /* min FW version that supports calibration */
 #define ELAN_MIN_CALIBRATION_FW 0x0138
 
